  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Brisk conditions expected through this weekend \nwith scattered mountain snow showers over the Alleghenies.\n\nWith the core of an upper low set up to our northeast, flow\naloft has now turned out of the NNW. The surface low is now well\noffshore, but the pressure gradient between this and an\napproaching surface high form the west will keep winds elevated\nthroughout the day today and Sunday. Scattered snow showers are\nbeing observed with a few streamers moving off of Lake Erie and\ninto our western mountains. Additional rounds of snow showers \nare expected at times through Sunday morning. Highest chances \nfor accumulation will likely be focused near western Garrett \nCounty and surrounding areas, with up to an additional 0.5\" \naccumulation possible.\n\nAs the broad system gradually tracks offshore, clouds may be slow to \nclear during the weekend until the start of next week. In the wake \nof the system's stemming cold front, temperatures will be noticeably \ncolder over the next several days along with gusty winds that will \npersist through the weekend, creating overall brisk conditions \noutside. Daily highs through the weekend range in the 40s and 50s, \nwith cooler temperatures over the mountains, as well as strong \nnorthwesterly winds gusting up to 20-30 knots at times, potentially \nstronger at high elevations. Overnight, temperatures range in the \n20s and 30s. Overall, expect nighttime freezing to near-freezing\ntemperatures to persist over the next several days, with winds \ndiminishing sometime between late Sunday and Monday.\n\nThe 2026 growing season has concluded. Therefore, no further\nFrost/Freeze headlines will be issued until Spring 2027.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions and below normal temperatures \ncontinue through Tuesday with warmer temperatures mid to late next \nweek.\n\nLooking at a quiescent weather pattern for much of the new \nworkweek. No hazardous weather is expected with dry conditions \nand moderating temperatures. The upper-level trough will exit \neast of the area Monday with a building upper-level ridge as \nsurface high pressure pushes east from the Ohio River Valley. \n1032-1036 mb high pressure will meander west of the area Monday \nbefore shunting south toward west-central VA and southwest VA \nmidweek. The high will drift offshore Thursday into Friday \nleading to further warming into the upcoming weekend as the \nridge continues to build.\n\nAs a result, expect winds to decrease substantially from what \nwe saw over the weekend as the high settles nearby. Temperatures\nwill remain below normal under north to northwesterly flow \nthrough Tuesday before winds shift to the west and south late \nnext week. Given the surface high nearby and lighter winds at \nthe surface, expect several nights of ample radiational cooling.\nThis will lead to widespread lows near or below freezing \noutside of the urban centers and tidal shorelines.\n\nHigh temperatures will push back into the mid to upper 50s Monday \n(40s mountains), then mid to upper 60s late next week. Dry \nconditions will persist through the weekend despite a potential \ntrough/low pressure system developing in the vicinity of \nsoutheast U.S. coast.",
